Aerie-Purpose: Stop an extension from allowing cookies through chrome.contentSettings.cookies.set in a way that lets third-party cookies through while the user blocks them.
Aerie-Upstream-Area: chrome/browser/extensions/api/content_settings/content_settings_api.cc (ContentSettingsContentSettingSetFunction::Run)
Aerie-Kind: hook

Patch 0006 locks chrome.privacy.websites.thirdPartyCookiesAllowed, but an extension with the
contentSettings permission could still set an ALLOW or SESSION_ONLY cookie exception. An
explicit exception wins over the third-party block (cookie_settings_base.cc,
kAllowByExplicitSetting), and a pattern such as *://*.com/* covers whole domains. The set
function now returns an error for a cookie ALLOW or SESSION_ONLY setting, in either scope,
only when the cookie settings of that profile block third-party cookies
(CookieSettings::ShouldBlockThirdPartyCookies, so it follows the user's choice in settings)
and the exception could apply to a third-party context. An exception applies to the first
party only when the secondary pattern equals the primary pattern and that pattern names one
host (it is not <all_urls> and has no domain wildcard); such a first-party allow is
accepted, so an extension that keeps a cookie whitelist for its own sites keeps working.
Blocking cookies, reading, getting and clearing, and the other content setting types are
not changed. When the user allows third-party cookies, nothing is refused. An exception that
an extension sets while the user allows third-party cookies stays in force if the user later
blocks them again.

--- a/chrome/browser/extensions/api/content_settings/content_settings_api.cc
+++ b/chrome/browser/extensions/api/content_settings/content_settings_api.cc
@@ -320,6 +320,31 @@
     return RespondNow(Error(extension_misc::kIncognitoSessionOnlyErrorMessage));
   }
 
+  // Aerie: while third-party cookies are blocked, an extension cannot allow
+  // cookies (or allow them for the session) for a pattern that could apply to
+  // a third-party context, because an explicit allow wins over the block. An
+  // allow whose secondary pattern equals its primary pattern, naming one
+  // host, only applies to that site's own cookies, so it stays possible.
+  if (content_type == ContentSettingsType::COOKIES &&
+      (setting == CONTENT_SETTING_ALLOW ||
+       setting == CONTENT_SETTING_SESSION_ONLY)) {
+    Profile* cookie_profile = Profile::FromBrowserContext(browser_context());
+    if (incognito) {
+      cookie_profile =
+          cookie_profile->GetPrimaryOTRProfile(/*create_if_needed=*/true);
+    }
+    const bool first_party_only = secondary_pattern == primary_pattern &&
+                                  !primary_pattern.MatchesAllHosts() &&
+                                  !primary_pattern.HasDomainWildcard();
+    if (!first_party_only &&
+        CookieSettingsFactory::GetForProfile(cookie_profile)
+            ->ShouldBlockThirdPartyCookies()) {
+      return RespondNow(Error(
+          "An extension cannot allow cookies for this pattern while "
+          "third-party cookies are blocked. Only the user can, in settings."));
+    }
+  }
+
   scoped_refptr<ContentSettingsStore> store =
       ContentSettingsService::Get(browser_context())->content_settings_store();
   store->SetExtensionContentSetting(extension_id(), primary_pattern,
